<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Countries &amp; <em>nationalities</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">In a voluntary introduction, someone may ask <span class="ex">Where are you from?</span> You can answer with a <strong>country</strong>, <span class="ex">I'm from Brazil</span>, or use a <strong>nationality adjective</strong>, <span class="ex">I'm Brazilian</span>. Keep country, nationality, language, and current home separate, and use only information that a real person chooses to provide.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">country · nationality · language</span>
    <span class="tag">from + country · be + adjective</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional practice</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Country versus nationality.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Group nationalities by ending.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Match, choose, and build sentences.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Share or ask only when the context is comfortable.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">Use a <strong>capital letter</strong> for country names, nationality words, and language names: <span class="ex">Spain · Spanish · Japan · Japanese · Portuguese</span>, never <span class="ex">spain</span>, <span class="ex">spanish</span>, or <span class="ex">portuguese</span>.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved question order with are, a country after from, and a nationality adjective after am.">
    <h3>Bring earlier grammar into an introduction</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the complete context. Read each finished sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier grammar choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are">are</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is">is</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Brazil">Brazil</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Brazilian">Brazilian</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Where <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are" data-hint="With you, place are before the subject in this question." data-fix="Where are you from?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> you from?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional profile says, “I'm from <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Brazil" data-hint="After from, choose the country name Brazil." data-fix="I'm from Brazil." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The same profile says, “I'm <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Brazilian" data-hint="After am, choose the nationality adjective Brazilian." data-fix="I'm Brazilian." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Meet the countries</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Each flag represents the listed <strong>country</strong>. Use the visible written label rather than identifying a country from color or symbol alone. The second word is a <strong>nationality adjective</strong> used in a sentence such as <span class="ex">She's Mexican</span>. A flag does not prove a person's nationality, language, birthplace, or identity.</p>
  <div class="figure-grid">
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/mx.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Mexico<small>Mexican</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/jp.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Japan<small>Japanese</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/br.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Brazil<small>Brazilian</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/es.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Spain<small>Spanish</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/it.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Italy<small>Italian</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/fr.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">France<small>French</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/tr.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Türkiye<small>Turkish</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/de.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Germany<small>German</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/us.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">United States<small>American</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/gb.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">United Kingdom<small>British</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/ca.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Canada<small>Canadian</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/cn.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">China<small>Chinese</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/in.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">India<small>Indian</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/kr.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">South Korea<small>Korean</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/ru.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Russia<small>Russian</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/pl.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Poland<small>Polish</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/eg.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Egypt<small>Egyptian</small></span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img class="flag" src="/assets/flags/pt.svg" alt="" width="66" height="50" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">Portugal<small>Portuguese</small></span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Two ways to answer one question</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">I'm from Brazil. &nbsp;→&nbsp; I'm Brazilian.</span><br /><span class="ex">I'm from Japan. &nbsp;→&nbsp; I'm Japanese.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which word names the <strong>country</strong>, the place? Which nationality word describes where the person is from?</li>
      <li>After <span class="ex">from</span>, do we use the country or the nationality? And after <span class="ex">am / is / are</span>?</li>
      <li>Look at the endings: <span class="ex">Brazil → Brazil<strong>ian</strong></span>, <span class="ex">Japan → Japan<strong>ese</strong></span>. The nationality is built <strong>from</strong> the country. What changed?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. From introduces the country Mexico or Italy, while am and is introduce the nationality adjectives Mexican or Italian.">
    <h3>Discovery check: from or be?</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">After <b>from</b> use the country. After <b>am / is / are</b> use the nationality.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Mexico">Mexico</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Mexican">Mexican</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Italy">Italy</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Italian">Italian</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional speaker says, “I'm from <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Mexico" data-hint="After from, use the country name Mexico." data-fix="I'm from Mexico." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The same speaker says, “I'm <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Mexican" data-hint="After am, use the nationality adjective Mexican." data-fix="I'm Mexican." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The profile states that Lena uses <span class="ex">she</span> and is from Italy. She's <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Italian" data-hint="After is, use the nationality adjective Italian." data-fix="She's Italian." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Lena and Amir both state that they are from Italy. They're from <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Italy" data-hint="After from, use the country name Italy." data-fix="They're from Italy." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: from a country, be a nationality</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>There are two simple patterns. Keep these chunks together:</p>
  <p><strong>1. from + country.</strong> &nbsp;<span class="ex">Where are you from? &nbsp;→&nbsp; I'm from Spain.</span> This answer gives a place connected to the speaker.</p>
  <p><strong>2. be + nationality adjective.</strong> &nbsp;<span class="ex">I'm Spanish. &nbsp;She's Spanish. &nbsp;They're Spanish.</span> The adjective describes nationality and does not change for gender or number.</p>
  <p>Do not add <span class="ex">a</span> or <span class="ex">an</span> before the adjective in this pattern: <span class="ex">I'm Brazilian</span>. Some nationality words can also be person nouns, as in <span class="ex">a Brazilian</span>, but those noun patterns vary. The dependable A0 pattern is <span class="ex">a Brazilian person</span> or <span class="ex">two Brazilian people</span>.</p>
  <p>Many nationality adjectives use common endings, but an ending is only a memory aid. It is not a rule that can generate every word. Learn each country and nationality adjective as a pair:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>ending</th><th>country</th><th>nationality</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>-ian / -an</b></td><td>Brazil · Italy · Canada · Egypt · Colombia</td><td>Brazil<b>ian</b> · Ital<b>ian</b> · Canad<b>ian</b> · Egypt<b>ian</b> · Colomb<b>ian</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>-ish</b></td><td>Türkiye · Spain · Poland</td><td>Turk<b>ish</b> · Span<b>ish</b> · Pol<b>ish</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>-ese</b></td><td>Japan · China · Portugal</td><td>Japan<b>ese</b> · Chin<b>ese</b> · Portugu<b>ese</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>other common pairs</b></td><td>Mexico · Germany · the United States · France</td><td><b>Mexican</b> · <b>German</b> · <b>American</b> · <b>French</b></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Country, nationality, language, and current home are different</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>meaning</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>country connected to the person</td><td><span class="ex">Mina is from Brazil.</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>nationality adjective the person uses</td><td><span class="ex">Mina is Brazilian.</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>language the person says they speak</td><td><span class="ex">Mina speaks Portuguese and English.</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>current home</td><td><span class="ex">Mina lives in Canada now.</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>One fact does not prove the others. A Brazilian person may speak Portuguese, another language, or several languages. Someone can be from one country, have more than one nationality, and live in another country. Use the words a person provides. Do not infer identity from a name, appearance, accent, or flag.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Small country-name boundaries</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Use <span class="ex">the</span> in <span class="ex">the United States</span> and <span class="ex">the United Kingdom</span>: <span class="ex">She's from the United States.</span> Most names here have no article: <span class="ex">from Mexico · from Japan · from Brazil</span>.</li>
      <li><span class="ex">The United Kingdom</span> includes England, Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland. <span class="ex">British</span> and <span class="ex">English</span> are not exact synonyms.</li>
      <li>In English, <span class="ex">American</span> commonly names a nationality connected to the United States. <span class="ex">The Americas</span> is a larger geographic region with many countries.</li>
      <li>The official country name used here is <span class="ex">Türkiye</span>. You may still hear or read <span class="ex">Turkey</span> in some English-language contexts. The nationality adjective is <span class="ex">Turkish</span>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each complete profile separates a country, nationality adjective, language, or current home instead of treating them as the same fact.">
    <h3>Meaning check: which kind of word?</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Read the label and the whole profile sentence before choosing.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Country, nationality, language, and home choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Brazil">Brazil</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Brazilian">Brazilian</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Portuguese">Portuguese</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Canada">Canada</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Country connected to Mina: She is from <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Brazil" data-hint="After from, choose the country Brazil." data-fix="Mina is from Brazil." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Nationality adjective Mina uses: She is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Brazilian" data-hint="After is, choose the nationality adjective Brazilian." data-fix="Mina is Brazilian." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>One language Mina says she speaks: She speaks <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Portuguese" data-hint="The sentence asks for a language, not a country or nationality." data-fix="Mina speaks Portuguese." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Current home in this fictional profile: She lives in <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Canada" data-hint="Lives in identifies the current home given by the profile." data-fix="Mina lives in Canada now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> now.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: the strong beat can move</h3>
    <p>When a country becomes a nationality, the number of syllables or the main stress can change. Learn the pair aloud, not only by spelling.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>country</th><th>nationality</th><th>stress pattern</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Mexico</td><td>Mexican</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>MEX</strong>-i-co → <strong>MEX</strong>-i-can</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Italy</td><td>Italian</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>IT</strong>-a-ly → i-<strong>TAL</strong>-ian</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Japan</td><td>Japanese</td><td><span class="ex">Ja-<strong>PAN</strong> → Jap-a-<strong>NESE</strong></span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>China</td><td>Chinese</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>CHI</strong>-na → Chi-<strong>NESE</strong></span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Brazil</td><td>Brazilian</td><td><span class="ex">Bra-<strong>ZIL</strong> → Bra-<strong>ZIL</strong>-ian</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The ending <span class="ex">-ese</span> carries a strong beat in <span class="ex">Japanese</span>, <span class="ex">Chinese</span>, and <span class="ex">Portuguese</span>. In a sentence, make the place or nationality clear: <span class="ex">I’m from <strong>MEX</strong>ico. I’m <strong>MEX</strong>ican.</span></p>
    <p>In relaxed U.S. speech, <span class="ex">Where are you from?</span> may connect and sound close to <span class="ex">Where-er-yuh from?</span>. <span class="ex">I'm from</span> often has a light, quick <span class="ex">from</span>. Recognize the connected form, but keep the full words in writing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Meaning check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Can we say <span class="ex">I'm from Brazilian</span>? <strong>No.</strong> After <em>from</em> we name the country: <span class="ex">I'm from Brazil.</span></li>
      <li>Can we say <span class="ex">I'm Brazil</span>? <strong>No.</strong> After <em>be</em> we use the nationality: <span class="ex">I'm Brazilian.</span></li>
      <li>Do we write <span class="ex">spanish</span> with a small letter? <strong>No.</strong> Countries and nationalities always take a <strong>capital</strong>: <span class="ex">Spain, Spanish</span>.</li>
      <li>Is the nationality the same for he, she, and they? <strong>Yes.</strong> The adjective never changes: <span class="ex">He's French. She's French. They're French.</span></li>
      <li>Does <span class="ex">She's from Brazil</span> prove which language she speaks or where she lives now? <strong>No.</strong> Ask or use only information she provides.</li>
      <li>Does <span class="ex">Where are you from?</span> always mean current home? <strong>No.</strong> For current home, ask <span class="ex">Where do you live now?</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read country, nationality, and language listening check">
    <h2>Listen: place, nationality, or language?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional profile sentence once at a natural speed and once more slowly. Listen before you choose.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You heard countries after from, nationality adjectives after be, a language after speaks, and the article in the United States.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Listening choices">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Brazil">Brazil</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Brazilian">Brazilian</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Japan">Japan</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Japanese">Japanese</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Portuguese">Portuguese</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Portugal">Portugal</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the">the</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="no article">no article</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Italian">Italian</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Italy">Italy</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Canada">Canada</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Canadian">Canadian</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Diego's fictional profile says, “I'm from <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Brazil" data-hint="Listen for the country after from." data-fix="I'm from Brazil." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.”</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Akira's profile says, “I'm <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Japanese" data-hint="Listen for the nationality adjective after am." data-fix="I'm Japanese." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.”</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Rita says she speaks <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Portuguese" data-hint="Listen for the language after speaks." data-fix="Rita says she speaks Portuguese." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Omar's profile says, “I live in <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the" data-hint="The complete country name the United States includes the." data-fix="I live in the United States." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> United States.”</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Giulia's profile states that she is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Italian" data-hint="Listen for the nationality adjective, not the country Italy." data-fix="Giulia is Italian." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Noah's profile states that he is from <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Canada" data-hint="Listen for the country name after from." data-fix="Noah is from Canada." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>Teacher: open the script after the learner checks</summary>
      <ol><li>Diego's fictional profile says, “I'm from Brazil.”</li><li>Akira's profile says, “I'm Japanese.”</li><li>Rita says she speaks Portuguese.</li><li>Omar's profile says, “I live in the United States.”</li><li>Giulia's profile states that she is Italian.</li><li>Noah's profile states that he is from Canada.</li></ol>
    </details>
  </section>

  <h2>Build the pairs</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>

  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-game data-success-message="Correct. Each match preserves the learned country and nationality-adjective pair rather than guessing from an ending alone.">
    <h3>Drill 1: match each country to its nationality</h3>
    <p class="tile-target">Tap a country, then tap the nationality that fits it</p>
    <div class="tile-bank">
      <button class="tile" data-tile="brazil">Brazil</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="japan">Japan</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="turkiye">Türkiye</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="mexico">Mexico</button>
    </div>
    <div class="slot-board">
