  <h2>Use it in real life</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to share your nationality, birthplace, current home, languages, immigration history, or information about real coworkers, clients, family, or friends. Use a fictional profile or a public figure's verified self-description. Ask about origin only when the setting is appropriate and the person appears comfortable sharing.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>A fictional profile:</b> Choose or invent a country, nationality adjective, language, and current home. Keep the four facts separate: <span class="ex">Mina is from Brazil. She is Brazilian. She speaks Portuguese. She lives in Canada now.</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Two possible answers:</b> A fictional speaker answers in two ways: <span class="ex">Where are you from? I'm from Mexico. I'm Mexican.</span> Add <span class="ex">And you?</span> only if the other person is participating voluntarily.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Current home, not origin:</b> Contrast <span class="ex">Where are you from?</span> with <span class="ex">Where do you live now?</span> Give different invented answers and explain which question asks about the current home.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Information gap:</b> You and your teacher each choose one fictional profile. Ask for one missing fact at a time. Do not guess a nationality or language from the person's name, picture, or accent.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Repair an assumption:</b> Your partner says, <span class="ex">She's from Brazil, so she speaks Portuguese.</span> Respond with the known fact and the missing information: <span class="ex">She's from Brazil. We don't know which languages she speaks.</span></div>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: welcome a fictional international team</h3>
    <p>Create three short profile cards and give a 45- to 60-second welcome. Use labels or invented information rather than real private details.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Use <span class="ex">from + country</span> accurately at least twice.</li>
      <li>Use <span class="ex">be + nationality adjective</span> accurately at least twice.</li>
      <li>Include <span class="ex">the</span> if you use the United States or the United Kingdom.</li>
      <li>Keep nationality, language, and current home separate.</li>
      <li>Ask one correctly ordered, respectful question and accept “I'd rather not say.”</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Close the level</h3>
    <p>Complete this aloud: “After <strong>from</strong>, I use the ______ . After <strong>am, is, are</strong>, I use the nationality ______ . Country, nationality, and language words begin with a ______ letter.”</p>
    <p>Then explain why <span class="ex">She's from Brazil</span> does not tell you her language or current home. Give one fictional example with different origin and current-home countries. This is the last A0 lesson in the sequence. The A0 level check provides broader evidence across several skills.</p>
  </div>

